<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<odoo>
    <data noupdate="1">
        <!--
            Least-privilege group for the tenant scheduler account (#347).

            THE PROBLEM THIS SOLVES. Binding the anomaly crons to a real,
            non-superuser identity is what makes Ring-2 licence enforcement
            apply to them at all — `env.su` bypasses Odoo's entire access-check
            machinery, so a superuser cron could never be denied. But a plain
            base.group_user cannot CREATE ncollection.alert: the only row
            granting create was base.group_system, and the scheduler must not be
            a system user (LicenseEnforcementMixin exempts `_is_system()` as
            well as su, so that would reintroduce the bug it exists to close).

            Without this group the fix "worked" in the worst way: the licence
            bypass closed, every detected anomaly discarded on every plan, and
            correct/broken indistinguishable because both look like no alerts.
            Caught by two reviewers; the branch failed its own CI traceback gate.

            SCOPE, DELIBERATELY NARROW. Create and write on ncollection.alert
            and nothing else. Not unlink: alerts are resolved, never deleted by
            the scheduler. This is the same shape as group_config_sync beside
            it — a service account gets exactly the one permission its job
            needs, never base.group_system.

            ON THE UNRESTRICTED ALERT READ, raised in review. alert_security.xml
            defines four ir.rule records and all four are GROUP-scoped, none
            global. Odoo's _get_rules selects rules whose group the user belongs
            to; an empty set means Domain.AND([]) = TRUE, i.e. NO restriction,
            not deny-all. So this group reads all four alert categories rather
            than a slice.

            Accepted, deliberately, for two reasons that are checked rather than
            assumed: the account cannot authenticate at all (no password, no API
            key — pinned by test_the_scheduler_account_cannot_authenticate), and
            the digest never reads as itself, it switches to each recipient with
            with_user() before selecting their slice. So there is no path by
            which this read reaches a human who should not see it. It is scoped
            this way because a scheduler that could not read alerts could not
            deduplicate them.

            READ ON THE DETECTOR MODELS is granted too, but NOT here and not as
            app-user groups. hooks.py::_sync_scheduler_read_access adds
            read-only (1,0,0,0) rows for exactly sale.order, account.move,
            hr.attendance, stock.quant and stock.warehouse.orderpoint, only for
            those a given database actually has. In code rather than in the CSV
            because a CSV row cannot be conditional on a module being installed.

            (This paragraph used to say the group granted NO business read at
            all. That was true when written and became false the moment those
            ACLs were added — the same stale-comment problem this ticket keeps
            producing. Ring 2 still gates every one of those reads by plan,
            which is the entire point of #347.)
        -->
        <record id="group_cron_service" model="res.groups">
            <field name="name">NCollection Scheduler (service)</field>
            <field name="comment">Non-interactive tenant service account: may
                record anomaly alerts produced by scheduled detection. Not a
                system user, so plan entitlement still applies to everything it
                reads. Not for human users.</field>
        </record>
    </data>
</odoo>
